  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nAs of 2 PM Tuesday afternoon, numerous showers and isolated \nthunderstorms are ongoing across the central portions of KS. These \nshowers and thunderstorms are primarily due to a lead, midlevel \nshortwave trough lifting through eastern CO/western KS. A tropical \nairmass continues to advect into the region with PW values approach \n2\", especially across central and south-central KS. Thus far, hourly \nrainfall rates have ranged from 0.25-0.75\". Unless we see an uptick \nin convective elements within this broad precipitation shield, these \nrates should hold throughout the afternoon hours. As the shortwave \ntrough lifts into NE later this afternoon/evening, weak subsidence \nin its wake should decrease coverage of shower/storm activity, at \nleast briefly. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms will continue into the nighttime hours \nTuesday night into Wednesday morning as strong low/midlevel WAA \noverlap with increasing midlevel height falls. Latest CAM and global \nguidance has slightly shifted the heaviest axis of rainfall \nsoutheast and may encompass more of central KS. The abnormally moist \nairmass will continue to support heavy rainfall with rates exceeding \n1\" per hour possible, especially with any convective elements. The \nmain midlevel low will eject into the central Plains Wednesday \nafternoon with continued rounds of heavy rainfall as chances spread \neastwards into southeast KS. In fact, the potential for a few severe \nstorms will increase Wednesday afternoon across central and south-\ncentral KS. A Pacific front/dryline will progress across the area \nduring the afternoon hours. The prefrontal environment will remain \nvery moist with lapse rates up to 7 C/km resulting in instability of \n500-1000 J/kg. Shear, on the other hand, will be quite impressive \nwith long, veering hodographs throughout the profile (~40 kt of \neffective shear). Should this environment emerge, a few supercells \nwould become likely with large hail, damaging winds, and even \ntornadoes possible. \n\nStrong midlevel height falls combined with right exit jet dynamics \nshould support widespread rainfall late Wednesday into Thursday \nmorning. This axis should setup across portions eastern OK into \neastern KS. As the midlevel low/trough slides through the area late \nThursday into Thursday night, deformation precipitation will \nprogress through the entire area. The greatest rain totals are still \nforecast to remain across portions of central and southeast KS. \nWidespread totals up to 3\" with locally higher amounts are expected. \nThe Flood Watch remains unchanged although short term trends will \nneed to be monitored for an expansion towards the I-135 corridor. \n\nFor Friday and beyond, midlevel heights will increase as western \nCONUS ridge axis builds into the Plains. This should yield drier \nconditions but seasonable temperatures in the 70s.",
